---
import ProjectLayout from "../../layouts/ProjectLayout.astro";

const layers = [
  { label: "Product", items: ["Character builder", "Campaign hub", "DM table", "Mobile sheet"] },
  { label: "Application", items: ["Reflex state", "Rules math", "Email verification", "Role boundaries"] },
  { label: "Data", items: ["SQLite local", "PostgreSQL production", "319-spell SRD", "Persistent campaigns"] },
  { label: "Delivery", items: ["pytest", "Docker", "Cloud Run", "Automated builds"] },
];
---

<ProjectLayout
  title="DND and Beyond"
  description="A personal full-stack Python project I built to demonstrate my technical range through a 5e-compatible character and campaign application, from calculated sheets and local rules data to production deployment."
  eyebrow="Full-stack Python application"
  emoji="🐉"
  repo="https://github.com/GlassHouse97/DND-And-Beyond"
  accent="#a8b4ff"
  showStats={false}
>
  <div slot="hero-visual" class="campaign-card" aria-label="DND and Beyond product capabilities">
    <div class="campaign-top">
      <span>CAMPAIGN / EMBERFALL</span>
      <small>DM view</small>
    </div>
    <div class="campaign-body">
      <div class="session-note">
        <small>Where we left off</small>
        <strong>The party reached the ruined watchtower.</strong>
        <p>Shared notes, DM state, party HP, conditions, NPCs, and initiative persist between sessions.</p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </div>

  <section class="project-section">
    <div><span class="section-number">01</span><h2>The problem</h2></div>
    <div class="body-copy">
      <p>Tabletop tools tend to split the experience across character sheets in one place, campaign notes in another, and the game master tracking initiative somewhere else.</p>
      <p>
        I wanted one phone-friendly Python application where players could manage calculated characters, join campaigns with invite codes, and return to a shared campaign state while the game master gets a purpose-built table for notes, NPCs, initiative, and party status.
      </p>
      <div class="callout"><strong>The product bar.</strong> Exact rules math, useful mobile views, persistent campaign state, and a DM screen that opens directly onto “where we last left off.”</div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="project-section">
    <div><span class="section-number">02</span><h2>What I built</h2></div>
    <div class="body-copy">
      <p>The project grew from a character sheet into a real multi-user product with explicit player and game-master workflows.</p>
      <div class="feature-grid">
        <article><span aria-hidden="true">🪪</span><h3>Identity</h3><p>Email registration, verification, login, and blank-by-default user dashboards.</p></article>
        <article><span aria-hidden="true">⚔️</span><h3>Characters</h3><p>Calculated sheets with abilities, saves, skills, combat stats, attacks, equipment, and spells.</p></article>
        <article><span aria-hidden="true">🗺️</span><h3>Campaigns</h3><p>Invite codes, roles, shared notes, session logs, character attachments, and party state.</p></article>
        <article><span aria-hidden="true">🎲</span><h3>DM table</h3><p>Private notes, NPC and initiative trackers, conditions, dice, and synchronized HP.</p></article>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="project-section">
    <div><span class="section-number">03</span><h2>System at a glance</h2></div>
    <div class="body-copy system-stack">
      {layers.map((layer, index) => (
        <div class="system-layer">
          <span>{String(index + 1).padStart(2, "0")}</span>
          <strong>{layer.label}</strong>
          <ul>{layer.items.map((item) => <li>{item}</li>)}</ul>
        </div>
      ))}
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="project-section">
    <div><span class="section-number">04</span><h2>Rules without a runtime dependency</h2></div>
    <div class="body-copy">
      <p>The application ships with the complete 319-spell SRD 5.1 catalog, so core character creation and play do not depend on a third-party rules API staying online.</p>
      <p>
        Class-aware spell logic covers prepared and known spellcasting, Wizard spellbooks, Pact Magic, Magical Secrets, and ancestry-based innate spells. Official derived-stat math is isolated and covered by automated tests.
      </p>
      <ul>
        <li>Local bundled rules data for dependable core flows</li>
        <li>Clear attribution and use of CC-BY-4.0 SRD content</li>
        <li>Rules math kept separate from interface state for easier testing</li>
      </ul>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="project-section">
    <div><span class="section-number">05</span><h2>From laptop to production</h2></div>
    <div class="body-copy">
      <p>Local development stays lightweight with SQLite. Production switches to PostgreSQL and runs as a single Docker container on Google Cloud Run.</p>
      <p>
        Runtime data, generated files, and source code stay deliberately separated. Credentials live outside Git, Cloud secrets are scoped to a dedicated runtime identity, and pushes to the main branch can trigger automated builds and deployment.
      </p>
      <div class="button-row project-buttons">
        <a class="button button-primary" href="https://github.com/GlassHouse97/DND-And-Beyond" target="_blank" rel="noreferrer">Explore the application <span aria-hidden="true">↗</span></a>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>
</ProjectLayout>

<style>
  .campaign-card {
    overflow: hidden;
    border: 1px solid rgba(184, 165, 255, 0.34);
    border-radius: 20px;
    background: #161323;
    box-shadow: 0 36px 90px rgba(0, 0, 0, 0.48);
    transform: rotate(1.5deg);
  }

  .campaign-top {
    display: flex;
    justify-content: space-between;
    padding: 1rem 1.2rem;
    border-bottom: 1px solid rgba(255, 255, 255, 0.08);
    color: var(--purple);
    font-family: var(--font-mono);
    font-size: 0.68rem;
    letter-spacing: 0.1em;
  }

  .campaign-top small {
    color: #858095;
    letter-spacing: 0;
  }

  .campaign-body {
    padding: 1.2rem;
  }

  .session-note {
    padding: 1.35rem;
    border: 1px solid rgba(255, 255, 255, 0.08);
    border-radius: 14px;
    background: rgba(255, 255, 255, 0.03);
  }

  .session-note small {
    color: #8b8699;
    font-family: var(--font-mono);
    font-size: 0.62rem;
    letter-spacing: 0.08em;
    text-transform: uppercase;
  }

  .session-note strong {
    display: block;
    margin-block: 1.4rem 0.7rem;
    font-family: var(--font-display);
    font-size: 1.5rem;
    font-weight: 500;
    line-height: 1.2;
  }

  .session-note p {
    margin: 0;
    color: #9993a8;
    font-size: 0.82rem;
  }

  .feature-grid {
    display: grid;
    grid-template-columns: 1fr 1fr;
    gap: 12px;
    margin-top: 2rem;
  }

  .feature-grid article {
    padding: 1.4rem;
    border: 1px solid var(--line);
    border-radius: 15px;
    background: rgba(255, 255, 255, 0.024);
  }

  .feature-grid article > span {
    font-size: 1.35rem;
  }

  .feature-grid h3 {
    margin: 1.3rem 0 0.55rem;
    font-size: 1rem;
  }

  .feature-grid p {
    margin: 0;
    font-size: 0.88rem;
  }

  .system-stack {
    max-width: none !important;
  }

  .system-layer {
    display: grid;
    grid-template-columns: 38px 120px 1fr;
    gap: 1rem;
    align-items: start;
    padding: 1.2rem;
    border: 1px solid var(--line);
    border-bottom: 0;
    background: rgba(255, 255, 255, 0.02);
  }

  .system-layer:first-child {
    border-radius: 14px 14px 0 0;
  }

  .system-layer:last-child {
    border-bottom: 1px solid var(--line);
    border-radius: 0 0 14px 14px;
  }

  .system-layer > span {
    color: var(--purple);
    font-family: var(--font-mono);
    font-size: 0.68rem;
  }

  .system-layer > strong {
    font-size: 0.92rem;
  }

  .system-layer ul {
    display: flex;
    flex-wrap: wrap;
    gap: 7px;
    padding: 0;
    margin: 0;
    list-style: none;
  }

  .system-layer li {
    padding: 0.28rem 0.52rem;
    border: 1px solid var(--line);
    border-radius: 999px;
    font-size: 0.7rem;
  }

  .project-buttons {
    margin-top: 2rem;
  }

  @media (max-width: 620px) {
    .feature-grid {
      grid-template-columns: 1fr;
    }

    .system-layer {
      grid-template-columns: 34px 1fr;
    }

    .system-layer ul {
      grid-column: 1 / -1;
    }
  }
</style>
